#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192391 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192686 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9d0e7a8-77d1-58d7-b7f7-a820942ece69" :knowledge-point/title "Log-Likelihood Functions for Normal Random Variables"}] :question/id #uuid "19e7744e-9d87-5ad1-9648-029e7dc8e565" :question/math-academy-id "e-10113" :question/problem "Suppose $X_1,X_2,\\ldots,X_{8}$ is an I.I.D random sample with $X_i \\sim N(\\theta, 1^2),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown mean. For a particular sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_{8},$ you're given that \n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^{8} x_i = 11.8, \\qquad \n\\sum_{i=1}^{8} x_i^2 = 25.2.\n$$\n\nFind the log-likelihood function of the sample.\n\n*Hint: The probability density function for a normal random variable with mean $\\theta$ and standard deviation $1$ is given by* \n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi}} e^{-(x-\\theta)^2/2}, \\qquad x \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x; \\theta),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i};\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=8$ independent normal random variables with unknown mean $\\theta$ and variance $\\sigma^2 = 1,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x, \\theta) = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi}} e^{-(x-\\theta)^2/2}, \\qquad x \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).\n$$\n\nFirst, let's write down the log-density function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡f(x,\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{(x-\\theta )^{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{n}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{n}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{1}{2}({\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2}-2\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+n{\\theta }^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are told that $\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^{8} x_i = 11.8,$ $\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^{8} x_i^2 = 25.2,$ and $n=8.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =-\\frac{8}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{1}{2}(25.2-2\\theta \\cdot 11.8+8{\\theta }^{2}) \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-12.6+11.8\\theta -4{\\theta }^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192522 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192798 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7981ed99-ef47-5b86-9f92-bce4d45d9767" :knowledge-point/title "Log-Likelihood Functions for Exponential Random Variables"}] :question/id #uuid "f2a062b5-e42e-5c05-9bc1-cbbe7a10baac" :question/math-academy-id "e-11122" :question/problem "Suppose $X_1, X_2, X_3, X_4, X_5$ is an I.I.D random sample with $X_i \\sim \\text{Exp}(\\theta)$ and unknown rate parameter $\\theta.$ Find the log-likelihood function of the sample $2, \\, 4, \\, 1, \\, 3, \\, 7.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x; \\theta),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i};\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n = 5$ independent exponential random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x, \\theta) = \\theta e^{-\\theta x}, \\qquad x \\geq 0.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's write down the log-density function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡f(x,\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\theta {e}^{-\\theta x}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-\\theta x}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta {x}_{i}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =n\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} & =2+4+1+3+7=17\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $n = 5.$ Therefore,  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =5\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta \\cdot 17 \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -17\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192524 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192800 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cce53189-83e8-5ab9-9abc-cb159f761f55" :knowledge-point/title "Log-Likelihood Functions for Continuous Random Variables"}] :question/id #uuid "ac9e9734-fb34-59e4-bbe2-a2e55196f0c1" :question/math-academy-id "e-11124" :question/problem "Let $X_1, X_2, X_3,X_4$ be I.I.D. random variables with probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\theta x^{-\\theta-1}, \\qquad x \\geq 1,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter. Find the log-likelihood function of the sample $1.3, \\, 2.4, \\, 1.0,\\, 1.7.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x; \\theta),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i};\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=4$ independent random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\theta x^{-\\theta-1}, \\qquad x \\geq 1.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's write down the log-density function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡f(x;\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\theta {x}^{-\\theta -1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{-(\\theta +1)}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -(\\theta +1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -(\\theta +1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{i}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(\\theta +1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}1-(\\theta +1){\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{i} \\\\  & =n\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -(\\theta +1){\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor our sample, we have that $n=4,$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{i} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡1.3+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.4+\\operatorname{ln}⁡1.0+\\operatorname{ln}⁡1.7≈1.67.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ≈4\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -(\\theta +1)\\cdot 1.67 \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -1.67(\\theta +1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193199 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192798 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7981ed99-ef47-5b86-9f92-bce4d45d9767" :knowledge-point/title "Log-Likelihood Functions for Exponential Random Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "902ee92f-f1e4-5b40-ad45-fc0f0984c13a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191081} {:answer/id #uuid "11a2f3bd-741f-5731-abd0-7524e672a87c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-22.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191082} {:answer/id #uuid "2c428dd7-aca3-59aa-800a-c4a8f9e04ebe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "26.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191083} {:answer/id #uuid "49f15e7f-8246-5744-b09b-1ffcb2a7c235" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-20.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191084} {:answer/id #uuid "71b39c2e-386a-5eee-9485-d538eff24c0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-28.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191085}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "49f15e7f-8246-5744-b09b-1ffcb2a7c235" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-20.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191084} :answer-field/id #uuid "085d6d7c-e590-5e18-8b19-c31691226d38" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191080}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2d68b12a-1734-50e8-9980-7be41ad6608b" :question/math-academy-id "q-159834" :question/problem "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{8}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼\\text{Exp}(\\theta )$ and unknown rate parameter  $\\theta .$ For a particular sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{8},$ you're given that \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{8}{x}_{i}=28.4.\n$$\n\nGiven that the log-likelihood function of the sample is\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=a\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +b\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b?$\n\n*Hint: The probability density function for an exponential random variable is given by* \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\theta {e}^{-\\theta x},\\,x\\ge 0.\n$$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i};\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=8$ independent exponential random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x,\\theta )=\\theta {e}^{-\\theta x},\\,x\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nNext, we write down the log-density function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡f(x,\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\theta {e}^{-\\theta x}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-\\theta x}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta {x}_{i}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =n\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are told that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{8}{x}_{i}=28.4$ and $n=8.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =8\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta \\cdot 28.4 \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(-28.4)\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b=8+(-28.4)=-20.4.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526007 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192800 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cce53189-83e8-5ab9-9abc-cb159f761f55" :knowledge-point/title "Log-Likelihood Functions for Continuous Random Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b359b9b-c8e3-531f-a95f-0e32e5abb431" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21.34" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525348} {:answer/id #uuid "589aa7d6-1e51-5b22-b0d5-edc47f598f47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22.72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525433} {:answer/id #uuid "a6926b78-2aa4-511e-b5d2-d53f7a6f7c63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20.34" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525627} {:answer/id #uuid "c6044135-6246-58b4-bd3c-b8cbde4f68f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21.72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525708} {:answer/id #uuid "d7b931bf-511f-5425-8157-3221e14247cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19.72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525741}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c6044135-6246-58b4-bd3c-b8cbde4f68f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21.72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525708} :answer-field/id #uuid "c025da46-d257-5236-b4c3-eceb6426c181" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525939}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "374585a1-b6ca-5c27-b592-db80b72d2e1f" :question/math-academy-id "q-159905" :question/problem "Let ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{10}$ be I.I.D. random variables with probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\theta {x}^{-\\theta -1},\\,x\\ge 1,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter. For a particular sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{10},$ you're given that \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{10}\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{i}≈11.72.\n$$\n\nGiven that the log-likelihood function of the sample is\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=a\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -b(\\theta +1)\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $a+b,$ rounded to two decimal places?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i};\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=10$ independent random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\theta {x}^{-\\theta -1},\\,x\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nNext, we write down the log-density function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡f(x;\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\theta {x}^{-\\theta -1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{-(\\theta +1)}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -(\\theta +1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -(\\theta +1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{i}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -(\\theta +1){\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{i} \\\\  & =n\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -(\\theta +1){\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are told that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{10}\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{i}≈11.72$ and $n=10.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ≈10\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -(\\theta +1)\\cdot 11.72 \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -11.72(\\theta +1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b≈10+11.72=21.72.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526051 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192800 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cce53189-83e8-5ab9-9abc-cb159f761f55" :knowledge-point/title "Log-Likelihood Functions for Continuous Random Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c2dc518-cf0f-5c85-b5e9-472a8dde1e5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "31.86" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525266} {:answer/id #uuid "2719d2bd-32f9-5b1b-8fe3-b0674132b468" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "34.14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525300} {:answer/id #uuid "4ea594a6-2f07-5c57-b675-eb69bb3c7ddf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "35.86" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525404} {:answer/id #uuid "83277c3c-04a8-58ff-aa42-89421e8ac36a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "33.86" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525536} {:answer/id #uuid "97015a9e-a106-5e30-b5a1-3bf9ec2d9ab7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32.14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525594}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83277c3c-04a8-58ff-aa42-89421e8ac36a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "33.86" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525536} :answer-field/id #uuid "01d68fb1-bb3f-582f-8d7e-b5153f1072a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525847}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8b9c7eb4-449e-5e01-aa81-9a5a5e88c0e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-159911" :question/problem "Let ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{15}$ be I.I.D. random variables with probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\theta {x}^{-\\theta -1},\\,x\\ge 1,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter. For a particular sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{15},$ you're given that \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{15}\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{i}=18.86.\n$$\n\nGiven that the log-likelihood function of the sample is\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=a\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -b(\\theta +1)\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i};\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=15$ independent random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\theta {x}^{-\\theta -1},\\,x\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nNext, we write down the log-density function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡f(x;\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\theta {x}^{-\\theta -1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{-(\\theta +1)}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -(\\theta +1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -(\\theta +1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{i}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -(\\theta +1){\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{i} \\\\  & =n\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -(\\theta +1){\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are told that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{15}\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{i}≈18.86$ and $n=15.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ≈15\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -(\\theta +1)\\cdot 18.86 \\\\  & =15\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -18.86(\\theta +1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b≈15+18.86=33.86.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526029 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192800 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cce53189-83e8-5ab9-9abc-cb159f761f55" :knowledge-point/title "Log-Likelihood Functions for Continuous Random Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "798d635f-fae0-507e-b73c-0d2a30289a2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9.44" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525511} {:answer/id #uuid "8e981499-e7bd-5801-9931-238a3b1b8603" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.44" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525566} {:answer/id #uuid "d3ce25de-5448-5169-b032-249b3503aab9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10.17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525733} {:answer/id #uuid "e207a007-e89f-5a28-b688-bf93535b1edd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11.23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525766} {:answer/id #uuid "f79829d7-e601-53a2-9e7d-45e71cad5bd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12.17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525820}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3ce25de-5448-5169-b032-249b3503aab9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10.17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525733} :answer-field/id #uuid "52748e2c-b3ed-5f84-8520-ac22477d5fe0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525888}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "643be908-7887-5e6d-8262-f7b785e61769" :question/math-academy-id "q-159913" :question/problem "Let ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3},{X}_{4},{X}_{5}$ be I.I.D. random variables with probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\theta {x}^{-\\theta -1},\\,x\\ge 1,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter. The log-likelihood function of the sample $1.8,\\,2.4,\\,6.0,\\,3.1,\\,2.2$ is\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=a\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -b(\\theta +1).\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $a+b,$ rounded to two decimal places?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i};\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=5$ independent random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\theta {x}^{-\\theta -1},\\,x\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nNext, we write down the log-density function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡f(x;\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\theta {x}^{-\\theta -1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{-(\\theta +1)}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -(\\theta +1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -(\\theta +1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{i}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -(\\theta +1){\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{i} \\\\  & =n\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -(\\theta +1){\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{i} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡1.8+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.4+\\operatorname{ln}⁡6.0+\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.2≈5.17\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $n=5.$ Therefore,  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ≈5\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -(\\theta +1)\\cdot 5.17 \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -5.17(\\theta +1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b≈5+5.17=10.17.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526063 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192800 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cce53189-83e8-5ab9-9abc-cb159f761f55" :knowledge-point/title "Log-Likelihood Functions for Continuous Random Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ab2d50a-57c4-5e74-ba36-cf05be5ad968" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.53" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525260} {:answer/id #uuid "72ef5243-4005-58fe-99e6-c13a1b0aa76a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9.87" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525496} {:answer/id #uuid "81e1c08e-1d6d-5cb1-b21f-5d978fa29ff0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.46" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525533} {:answer/id #uuid "dca980d7-0b3b-5228-a822-2f0dff285bcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.53" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525753} {:answer/id #uuid "e4e80d8c-51a5-56c6-aea6-83c3a12d3e00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.87" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525772}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e4e80d8c-51a5-56c6-aea6-83c3a12d3e00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.87" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525772} :answer-field/id #uuid "2762c691-4dde-5bd4-af4f-e8ecd73e645e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525866}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a4ca9b4d-e32d-5442-96f5-2f609cd2145b" :question/math-academy-id "q-159915" :question/problem "Let ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3},{X}_{4}$ be I.I.D. random variables with probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\theta {x}^{-\\theta -1},\\,x\\ge 1,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter. The log-likelihood function of the sample $1.7,\\,2.4,\\,3.1,\\,1.4$ is\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=a\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -b(\\theta +1).\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $a+b,$ rounded to two decimal places?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i};\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=4$ independent random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\theta {x}^{-\\theta -1},\\,x\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nNext, we write down the log-density function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡f(x;\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\theta {x}^{-\\theta -1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{-(\\theta +1)}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -(\\theta +1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -(\\theta +1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{i}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -(\\theta +1){\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{i} \\\\  & =n\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -(\\theta +1){\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{i} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡1.7+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.4+\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡1.4≈2.87\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $n=4.$ Therefore,  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ≈4\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -(\\theta +1)\\cdot 2.87 \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -2.87(\\theta +1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b≈4+2.87=6.87.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526071 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192800 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cce53189-83e8-5ab9-9abc-cb159f761f55" :knowledge-point/title "Log-Likelihood Functions for Continuous Random Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d2bf444-13ef-5c11-959f-7c5d30e503c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525268} {:answer/id #uuid "415e06a7-a8ea-5cf8-9ca7-9cd7017b776b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.03" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525367} {:answer/id #uuid "811e5452-032b-5194-8be0-a94f36f2a854" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.03" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525530} {:answer/id #uuid "f223fe28-b90e-50c6-beff-22ab1408e721" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9.45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525809} {:answer/id #uuid "f9e9c253-596d-53d5-b5c1-380f18a6ccdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525826}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "811e5452-032b-5194-8be0-a94f36f2a854" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.03" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525530} :answer-field/id #uuid "919f4566-f4f9-5b53-8405-c0ab0c7437ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525919}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b1c528bb-28eb-5c70-b173-6d36e7f34b5d" :question/math-academy-id "q-159916" :question/problem "Let ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3},{X}_{4}$ be I.I.D. random variables with probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\theta {x}^{-\\theta -1},\\,x\\ge 1,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter. The log-likelihood function of the sample $1.3,\\,2.9,\\,3.3,\\,4.5$ is\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=a\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -b(\\theta +1).\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $a+b,$ rounded to two decimal places?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i};\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=4$ independent random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\theta {x}^{-\\theta -1},\\,x\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nNext, we write down the log-density function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡f(x;\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\theta {x}^{-\\theta -1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{-(\\theta +1)}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -(\\theta +1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -(\\theta +1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{i}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -(\\theta +1){\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{i} \\\\  & =n\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -(\\theta +1){\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{i} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡1.3+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.9+\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.3+\\operatorname{ln}⁡4.5≈4.03\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $n=4.$ Therefore,  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ≈4\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -(\\theta +1)\\cdot 4.03 \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -4.03(\\theta +1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b≈4+4.03=8.03.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526000 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192798 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7981ed99-ef47-5b86-9f92-bce4d45d9767" :knowledge-point/title "Log-Likelihood Functions for Exponential Random Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1850930b-8e69-5990-b4cf-b73a443272de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525254} {:answer/id #uuid "2464f990-3e4c-524d-9e75-cf3490cf606a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525295} {:answer/id #uuid "26d60cb0-c669-5ba5-8bdd-39c3ed98c9e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525298} {:answer/id #uuid "d13ba093-80c7-5419-a26f-b7a8920c2f53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525726} {:answer/id #uuid "e1602e4e-37c2-5934-9be4-8b066267660d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525764}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d13ba093-80c7-5419-a26f-b7a8920c2f53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525726} :answer-field/id #uuid "89791f6b-63fb-54c9-9267-5c2e7f525e4a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525913}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2e0fcfa7-37ec-568d-b873-d7a1d1d3286a" :question/math-academy-id "q-159927" :question/problem "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{6}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼\\text{Exp}(\\theta )$ and unknown rate parameter $\\theta .$ The log-likelihood function of the sample $4,\\,6,\\,9,\\,7,\\,2,\\,1$ is\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=a\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +b\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $a+b?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i};\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=6$ independent exponential random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x,\\theta )=\\theta {e}^{-\\theta x},\\,x\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nNext, we write down the log-density function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡f(x,\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\theta {e}^{-\\theta x}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-\\theta x}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta {x}_{i}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =n\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} & =4+6+9+7+2+1=29\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $n=6.$ Therefore,  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =6\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta \\cdot 29 \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(-29)\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b=6+(-29)=-23.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526089 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192798 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7981ed99-ef47-5b86-9f92-bce4d45d9767" :knowledge-point/title "Log-Likelihood Functions for Exponential Random Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1609352b-3017-5d9b-ad0a-c257977d57d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525250} {:answer/id #uuid "796fdfd2-d7f6-5a87-b012-0ff7c5ba461e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525510} {:answer/id #uuid "a02fa58f-95f1-5643-8ac1-a986460d15f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525616} {:answer/id #uuid "b254971b-c53b-5c39-82f4-576a7441f4fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525661} {:answer/id #uuid "d0bc4e2f-df9e-55e1-8107-96c8e16cc22e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525725}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a02fa58f-95f1-5643-8ac1-a986460d15f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525616} :answer-field/id #uuid "a54e8dae-35a0-5e2f-af7a-169cc07a84b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525929}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e67f295c-595a-59e1-89e6-05c919508212" :question/math-academy-id "q-159928" :question/problem "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3},{X}_{4},{X}_{5}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼\\text{Exp}(\\theta )$ and unknown rate parameter $\\theta .$ The log-likelihood function of the sample $6,\\,8,\\,2,\\,3,\\,5$ is\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=a\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +b\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $a+b?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i};\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=5$ independent exponential random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x,\\theta )=\\theta {e}^{-\\theta x},\\,x\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nNext, we write down the log-density function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡f(x,\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\theta {e}^{-\\theta x}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-\\theta x}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta {x}_{i}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =n\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} & =6+8+2+3+5=24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $n=5.$ Therefore,  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =5\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta \\cdot 24 \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(-24)\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b=5+(-24)=-19.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526006 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192798 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7981ed99-ef47-5b86-9f92-bce4d45d9767" :knowledge-point/title "Log-Likelihood Functions for Exponential Random Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c0385a3-fadd-5000-8769-4ca1affdaa42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525310} {:answer/id #uuid "44ff6c12-5e41-5d8a-a8ed-ce27d6d9a7ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-14.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525381} {:answer/id #uuid "6e7e3d10-700e-57d5-a6df-d26ed13a0570" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-11.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525486} {:answer/id #uuid "ebc2aae9-7b1f-56aa-9940-1acf6c5f6f20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525789} {:answer/id #uuid "f078c584-c35c-500f-beb3-9f20e83d79a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-17.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525802}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f078c584-c35c-500f-beb3-9f20e83d79a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-17.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525802} :answer-field/id #uuid "fbb3031c-a5cc-577f-8ba0-340b2c59edc7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525979}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "33ce93bc-9b71-578c-a5f0-f916b3a30de2" :question/math-academy-id "q-159929" :question/problem "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3},{X}_{4}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼\\text{Exp}(\\theta )$ and unknown rate parameter $\\theta .$ The log-likelihood function of the sample $3.1,\\,6.5,\\,4.8,\\,7.2$ is\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=a\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +b\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $a+b?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i};\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=4$ independent exponential random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x,\\theta )=\\theta {e}^{-\\theta x},\\,x\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nNext, we write down the log-density function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡f(x,\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\theta {e}^{-\\theta x}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-\\theta x}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta {x}_{i}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =n\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} & =3.1+6.5+4.8+7.2=21.6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $n=4.$ Therefore,  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta \\cdot 21.6 \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(-21.6)\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b=4+(-21.6)=-17.6.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526087 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192798 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7981ed99-ef47-5b86-9f92-bce4d45d9767" :knowledge-point/title "Log-Likelihood Functions for Exponential Random Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5f42301c-1538-5f0c-87b5-7f8ec6a53aa7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-45.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525447} {:answer/id #uuid "6a9ca59b-7d72-580e-8d95-f336d8a23b23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "42.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525479} {:answer/id #uuid "6d4f74b5-7c85-54b5-87d8-14ad0d084cc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "34.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525484} {:answer/id #uuid "8012df4b-d142-5aae-a514-96edbc1da64d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-32.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525523} {:answer/id #uuid "a1376290-d6f6-5a37-ab41-4a4cef20fe85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-31.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525618}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a1376290-d6f6-5a37-ab41-4a4cef20fe85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-31.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525618} :answer-field/id #uuid "28e3238b-51f4-5326-b09c-7d12a6047b17" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525868}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e462cf70-9c4a-5912-9b6b-fb55715b7efa" :question/math-academy-id "q-159934" :question/problem "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{7}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼\\text{Exp}(\\theta )$ and unknown rate parameter $\\theta .$ For a particular sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{7},$ you're given that \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{7}{x}_{i}=38.9.\n$$\n\nGiven that the log-likelihood function of the sample is\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=a\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +b\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b?$\n\n*Hint: The probability density function for an exponential random variable is given by* \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\theta {e}^{-\\theta x},\\,x\\ge 0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i};\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=7$ independent exponential random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x,\\theta )=\\theta {e}^{-\\theta x},\\,x\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nNext, we write down the log-density function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡f(x,\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\theta {e}^{-\\theta x}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-\\theta x}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta {x}_{i}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =n\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are told that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{7}{x}_{i}=38.9$ and $n=7.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =7\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta \\cdot 38.9 \\\\  & =7\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(-38.9)\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b=7+(-38.9)=-31.9.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526076 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192686 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9d0e7a8-77d1-58d7-b7f7-a820942ece69" :knowledge-point/title "Log-Likelihood Functions for Normal Random Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f03e200-f459-5f54-91af-c0316f151882" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40.93" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525237} {:answer/id #uuid "2cbc8dc4-a28d-5d75-b595-1ff52453fe44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-41.43" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525311} {:answer/id #uuid "8f499e9c-2722-5b53-a436-8a79c8e22bd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-45.44" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525567} {:answer/id #uuid "9e6790c7-0760-5f1f-9923-f4cc21141e24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "47.14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525608} {:answer/id #uuid "bdff6e45-6d14-52f6-9ef8-48fc08a6c75e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-48.93" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525694}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2cbc8dc4-a28d-5d75-b595-1ff52453fe44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-41.43" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525311} :answer-field/id #uuid "abf413ab-f439-5034-a9fc-a671901ce187" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525932}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bf364950-64c1-5b8d-a6e8-f3296448f536" :question/math-academy-id "q-159946" :question/problem "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{12}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼N(\\theta ,{1}^{2}),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown mean. For a particular sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{12},$ you're given that \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{12}{x}_{i}=40,\\,{\\sum }_{i=1}^{12}{x}_{i}^{2}=150.86.\n$$\n\nGiven that the log-likelihood function of the sample is\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )+a+b\\theta +c{\\theta }^{2},\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c?$\n\n*Hint: The probability density function for a normal random variable with mean $\\theta $ and standard deviation $1$ is given by* \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i};\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=12$ independent normal random variables with unknown mean $\\theta $ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=1,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x,\\theta )=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nNext, we write down the log-density function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡f(x,\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{(x-\\theta )^{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{n}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{n}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{1}{2}({\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2}-2\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+n{\\theta }^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are told that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{12}{x}_{i}=40,$ ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{12}{x}_{i}^{2}=150.86,$ and $n=12.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =-\\frac{12}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{1}{2}(150.86-2\\theta \\cdot 40+12{\\theta }^{2}) \\\\  & =-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )+(-75.43)+40\\theta +(-6){\\theta }^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b+c=(-75.43)+40+(-6)=-41.43.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526023 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192686 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9d0e7a8-77d1-58d7-b7f7-a820942ece69" :knowledge-point/title "Log-Likelihood Functions for Normal Random Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d5894a6-585a-5378-980a-d0b4918ebe65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "117.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525272} {:answer/id #uuid "1dc91232-f4fd-5500-9e9c-3f3b79bc8f90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident 