${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i};\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=14$ independent normal random variables with unknown mean $\\theta $ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=1,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x,\\theta )=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nNext, we write down the log-density function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡f(x,\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{(x-\\theta )^{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{n}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{n}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{1}{2}({\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2}-2\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+n{\\theta }^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are told that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{14}{x}_{i}=26.8,$ ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{14}{x}_{i}^{2}=264.8,$ and $n=14.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =-\\frac{14}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{1}{2}(264.8-2\\theta \\cdot 26.8+14{\\theta }^{2}) \\\\  & =-7\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )+(-132.4)+26.8\\theta +(-7){\\theta }^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b+c=(-132.4)+26.8+(-7)=-112.6.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526094 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192686 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9d0e7a8-77d1-58d7-b7f7-a820942ece69" :knowledge-point/title "Log-Likelihood Functions for Normal Random Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cbcbe41-0ec2-507f-b713-b0a22ebf25be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-46.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525232} {:answer/id #uuid "18d01814-974d-57f1-bae6-fc57d9703528" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "44.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525256} {:answer/id #uuid "4b6cbd50-cadd-58bc-b6d8-24ab0ca5678b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-45.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525394} {:answer/id #uuid "8b889d32-017a-5881-9bfb-7de05958c82e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-42.0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525553} {:answer/id #uuid "fd2e10fc-7183-59ec-a31c-a3543cf568bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "43.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525834}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b6cbd50-cadd-58bc-b6d8-24ab0ca5678b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-45.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525394} :answer-field/id #uuid "939abb92-670e-59cb-a71d-b99cbb16a598" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525920}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ed6e56e9-08e5-56f5-aaed-8bc6f1c64b9a" :question/math-academy-id "q-159957" :question/problem "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{10}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼N(\\theta ,{1}^{2}),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown mean. For a particular sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{10},$ you're given that \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{10}{x}_{i}=39.4,\\,{\\sum }_{i=1}^{10}{x}_{i}^{2}=159.8.\n$$\n\n \nGiven that the log-likelihood function of the sample is\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )+a+b\\theta +c{\\theta }^{2},\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c?$\n\n*Hint: The probability density function for a normal random variable with mean $\\theta $ and standard deviation $1$ is given by* \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i};\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=10$ independent normal random variables with unknown mean $\\theta $ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=1,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x,\\theta )=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nNext, we write down the log-density function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡f(x,\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{(x-\\theta )^{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{n}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{n}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{1}{2}({\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2}-2\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+n{\\theta }^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are told that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{10}{x}_{i}=39.4,$ ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{10}{x}_{i}^{2}=159.8,$ and $n=10.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =-\\frac{10}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{1}{2}(159.8-2\\theta \\cdot 39.4+10{\\theta }^{2}) \\\\  & =-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )+(-79.9)+39.4\\theta +(-5){\\theta }^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b+c=(-79.9)+39.4+(-5)=-45.5.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526065 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192686 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9d0e7a8-77d1-58d7-b7f7-a820942ece69" :knowledge-point/title "Log-Likelihood Functions for Normal Random Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7ad0b6a5-098c-594c-94c0-b01a3d56c227" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3.65" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525512} {:answer/id #uuid "92cb029c-2e0e-58e9-a991-e4b7a62dc7d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3.85" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525576} {:answer/id #uuid "d1e2a988-f449-5bd2-b1ac-257bb17dc719" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525729} {:answer/id #uuid "ddbd0104-0775-5e7a-9449-d441ee1dd8a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525755} {:answer/id #uuid "e653b8b9-6ffe-5e07-9219-9ad99825ec93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525774}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ad0b6a5-098c-594c-94c0-b01a3d56c227" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3.65" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525512} :answer-field/id #uuid "c198272d-0f95-5188-850b-39d8ecf53871" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525941}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a72b9dd5-5d41-5e15-9611-0fb245235173" :question/math-academy-id "q-159959" :question/problem "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼N(\\theta ,{1}^{2}),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown mean. The log-likelihood function of the sample $2.5,\\,0.2,\\,-1.1$ is\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=-1.5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )+a+b\\theta +c{\\theta }^{2}\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i};\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=3$ independent normal random variables with unknown mean $\\theta $ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=1,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x,\\theta )=\\frac{1}{\\sigma \\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-\\frac{1}{2}{(\\frac{x-\\theta }{\\sigma })}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nNext, we write down the log-density function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡f(x,\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{(x-\\theta )^{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{n}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{n}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{1}{2}({\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2}-2\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+n{\\theta }^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =2.5+0.2+(-1.1) \\\\  & =1.6 \\\\ C & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(2.5)^{2}+(0.2)^{2}+(-1.1)^{2} \\\\  & =7.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $n=3.$ Therefore,  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{1}{2}(7.5-2\\theta \\cdot (1.6)+3{\\theta }^{2}) \\\\  & =-1.5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )+(-3.75)+(1.6)\\theta +(-1.5){\\theta }^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b+c=-3.75+(1.6)+(-1.5)=-3.65.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526098 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192686 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9d0e7a8-77d1-58d7-b7f7-a820942ece69" :knowledge-point/title "Log-Likelihood Functions for Normal Random Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b8cbbfa-0513-54a9-9d49-2cc903fb24c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525351} {:answer/id #uuid "5b817416-7bee-53d6-880c-88f15c1f4a7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525439} {:answer/id #uuid "719d881d-0eec-5fa6-8eba-9657fa379b5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525493} {:answer/id #uuid "b79c3683-0e2b-564a-a127-6988653a841f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525677} {:answer/id #uuid "cde26b50-374c-52a6-a8bb-fd5d777ad336" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525717}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "719d881d-0eec-5fa6-8eba-9657fa379b5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525493} :answer-field/id #uuid "4fa74bc1-cbda-547d-ad3a-17b839fe05e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525886}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f4dba462-88c8-55e1-ad6f-09af7f02eff1" :question/math-academy-id "q-159962" :question/problem "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3},{X}_{4},{X}_{5}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼N(\\theta ,{1}^{2}),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown mean. The log-likelihood function of the sample $-1.1,\\,0.7,\\,-0.5,\\,-0.7,\\,-1.4$ is\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=-2.5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )+a+b\\theta +c{\\theta }^{2}\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i};\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=5$ independent normal random variables with unknown mean $\\theta $ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=1,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x,\\theta )=\\frac{1}{\\sigma \\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-\\frac{1}{2}{(\\frac{x-\\theta }{\\sigma })}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nNext, we write down the log-density function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡f(x,\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{(x-\\theta )^{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{n}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{n}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{1}{2}({\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2}-2\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+n{\\theta }^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =(-1.1)+0.7+(-0.5)+(-0.7)+(-1.4) \\\\  & =-3 \\\\ C & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(-1.1)^{2}+(0.7)^{2}+(-0.5)^{2}+(-0.7)^{2}+(-1.4)^{2} \\\\  & =4.4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $n=5.$ Therefore,  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =-\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{1}{2}(4.4-2\\theta \\cdot (-3)+5{\\theta }^{2}) \\\\  & =-2.5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )+(-2.2)+(-3)\\theta +(-2.5){\\theta }^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b+c=-2.2+(-3)+(-2.5)=-7.7.\n$$"}]}
